                <pb facs="0332" n="232" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg232"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.00/3299: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dodd</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-07-06T21:00:00+01:00">July 6, 1934—9
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 6—5:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>133. Department’s 82, July 1, noon. In the course of a conversation on other
                    topics, Minister of Foreign Affairs told me that there was but one conspiracy
                    (note some accounts have indicated three separate movements) with Roehm and
                    Schleicher as leaders, aimed at imprisoning whole Government including Hitler,
                    and seizing power. Originally planned to take effect August 1st, was advanced to
                    June 30th, noon, because conspirators learned that Hitler was informed.
                    Chancellor only became convinced of danger night of 28th and then resolved on
                    immediate suppression, sending for Goebbels. Minister stated that there were
                    written proofs of conspiracy which would shortly be published. He admitted that
                    numerous innocent persons had been arrested. He denied that any foreign power is
                    implicated. He alluded to terrific strain under which Chancellor and other
                    members of administration, including himself, had been during the past week.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dodd</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
